     ; (only the Jacobian analogue exists, Jacobian/Jacobian.v:144).
     ; The projective facts it was reaching for are proved in
     ; Group/CurveAdd/RcbProjectiveLaws.v.
     ; The hand-written WP pathfinder specs: superseded by the Rupicola
     ; route (CurveAddGeneralA*, CurveDoubleGeneralA*, CurveA3_*), which
     ; does compile and is what the emitted Rust comes from.  These three
     ; have never compiled -- at HEAD or since -- because their closure
     ; tail (this_mod' substitution + ring over the 40-op chain) does not
     ; terminate; scripts/logs/p256_g1_add_debug_notes.md records the
     ; fifteen defect classes found while executing them.  Excluded so a
     ; whole-tree build is meaningful.
     Curve.P256_G1_Add_Spec
     ; Secp256k1_G1_Add_Spec is the same WP route and fails the same way.
     ; Its Admitted at line 896 records the cause: unfolding
     ; BLS12_add_mont_spec yields MontgomeryCurveG1Equiv.three_b_mont
     ; where the rewrite expects MontgomeryCurveSpecs.three_b_mont, so
     ; the rewrite finds no subterm -- the file itself calls this the
     ; "same root cause as P256_G1_Add_Spec.v line 1034".  Unlike its
     ; siblings it compiled, by admitting that obligation, so it was
     ; shipping an admit inside the build.
     ; Nothing needs it: no .v Requires it, there is no secp256k1
     ; instantiation of the Rupicola route, and secp256k1-safe-rust is
     ; field-only (no group module), so no G1 operation ships for this
     ; curve at either level.  If secp256k1 G1 is wanted later, the
     ; principled route is a CurveAddGeneralA/CurveAdd instantiation --
     ; secp256k1 has a = 0, so RCB Algorithm 7 and the Algorithm 9
     ; doubling of PointDoubleA0.v apply directly.
     Curve.Secp256k1_G1_Add_Spec
     ; The two CurveSpecsEquiv files added 2026-04-17 have never
     ; compiled.  Both fail on fiat-crypto API drift: the reference
     ; WordByWordMontgomery.r' is gone, and `m` is now a positive where
     ; these expect a Z.  Their three siblings from 2026-04-13
     ; (Pallas, Secp256k1, Vesta) predate the drift and do build.
     ; Neither is needed.  BW6_761CurveSpecsEquiv has no consumer at
     ; all; P256CurveSpecsEquiv's only consumer is P256Curve_G1_bedrock,
     ; already excluded above for the same drift (it cites P256.three_b,
     ; absent from the current Curves/Weierstrass/P256.v).  Excluded so
     ; a whole-tree build is meaningful; delete once someone confirms
     ; the pasta/secp equivalence route is the one being kept.
